{
  "description": "RFC 6962 §2.1 Merkle Tree Hash vectors for Tzun batch anchoring. Leaves are the RAW 32 bytes of a record's canonicalDigest (hex-decoded, NOT the 64 ASCII hex characters). leafHash = SHA-256(0x00 || d). internalNode = SHA-256(0x01 || left || right) over raw 32-byte children. An odd trailing node is PROMOTED, never duplicated — equivalently, MTH(D[n]) splits at the largest power of two strictly less than n. auditPath is RFC 6962 §2.1.1 PATH(m, D[n]), ordered leaf-first/root-last, as bare lowercase hex with no position label: the side of each step is derived from (leafIndex, treeSize), which is what lets a stock Certificate Transparency verifier check these proofs unmodified.",
  "source": "Computed by scripts/generate-merkle-vectors.mjs, a from-scratch RECURSIVE transcription of RFC 6962 §2.1/§2.1.1. The SDK implementation it pins (src/attestation/merkle.ts) is ITERATIVE and level-by-level; the two are independent code paths that must agree. This is an independent pin in the sense of vectors/custody-entry-hash-vectors.json: scripts/regenerate-vectors.ts does not and must not rewrite this file. emptyTreeRoot is the universally published RFC 6962 constant (SHA-256 of the empty string) and can be checked against any CT implementation without reference to Tzun.",
  "emptyTreeRoot": {
    "note": "MTH({}) per the RFC. Tzun never anchors an empty batch — buildMerkleTree refuses empty input — but the constant is pinned because it is the one value every CT implementation agrees on, so it independently confirms the hash function and encoding before any Tzun-specific value is trusted.",
    "root": "e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855"
  },
  "leafDigestDerivation": "leafDigests[i] = sha256(utf8(\"tzun-merkle-vector-\" + i))",
